  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 950 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain tapers off by early Sunday morning along \nthe coast. Clear and cool inland. \n\nA mid/upper-level trough has made its way to the mid-Atlantic\ncoast this evening and will close off just offshore of the OBX\nby 12z Sunday. Meanwhile, 1030+ mb surface high pressure will\ncontinue to build in from the northwest overnight allowing for\nclear and cool conditions for much of the FA. Overnight lows \nwill drop into the 40s to near 50 along the immediate coast, \nwith upper 30s to lower 40s inland. A partly to mostly sunny \nand breezy Fall day is ahead for Sunday. Despite plenty of \nsunshine for most areas, highs only in the upper 50s to lower \n60s are forecast. As high pressure settles just NNW of the area \nSunday night, another cool night is in store with lows in the\nupr 30s Piedmont to low 50s coast, followed by highs in the 60s\non Monday.\n\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2...Breezy N to NE winds are expected through the\nmiddle of next week, especially along the immediate coast. A\nWind Advisory remains in effect into early Sunday for the NC \nOBX.\n\nThe tightening pressure gradient between low pressure offshore \nand the 1030+ mb high to our northwest will maintain northerly \nwinds of 15 to 20 mph inland, with gusts of 20 to 30 mph. Along \nthe coast, NE winds of 25 to 30 mph with gusts up to 45 mph \nwill continue into Sunday, highest across the NC Northern Outer\nBanks. Winds for inland Currituck and VA Beach are gusting to\n30-40 mph at most so have cancelled the Wind Advisory there.\n\nAs the cold front shifts farther offshore, confidence continues\nto increase that the coastal low/nor'easter will deepen further\nthrough the first half of next week. The low is forecast to \nmeander offshore of the Carolina/lower mid-Atlantic coast \nthrough at least midweek, resulting in persistent breezy \nconditions inland and prolonged windy conditions along the \nimmediate coast. N-NE gusts will average 20 to 30 mph inland and\n30 to 45 mph along the coast, highest over the Virginia Capes \nsouth to the NC Northern Outer Banks.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Forecast uncertainty remains early next week \nregarding the track and intensity of the nor'easter offshore. \nRegardless of model spread, a long-duration coastal flood event \nis expected. Coastal Flood Watches remain in effect for \nMonday's high tides across the lower Chesapeake Bay, lower James\nRiver, and the Atlantic coast of the Eastern Shore.\n\nModel guidance exhibits spread regarding cyclone intensity and \nplacement, particularly beyond Tuesday. With the cutoff low \ndetached from the primary jet stream and lacking an upstream \nkicker trough, probabilistic ensembles favor a system meandering\noffshore through midweek. Gusty N-NE winds will drive building \ntidal anomalies over the next few days.\n\nProlonged onshore flow will drive anomalies to 1 to 3 feet \nabove normal astronomical tide. Fortunately, the region is \ncurrently in an astronomical neap tide cycle, which will help to\nmitigate peak water levels. Even so, widespread minor to \nmoderate coastal flooding is expected during high tides through \nearly next week, peaking on Monday. If the low strengthens \nfurther or edges closer to the coast, localized major flooding \ncannot be ruled out, though consensus guidance continues to \ntrend \"lower\" toward high-end minor to moderate thresholds.",
